        <section class="project-section">

            <div class="section-number">01</div>

            <div class="section-content">

                <h2>CHALLENGE</h2>

                <p>
                    Our starting point was a wicked problem:
                    <em>
                        "How might we support food-service businesses and their customers
                        to collectively reduce waste while balancing profitability,
                        convenience, and sustainability?"
                    </em>
                </p>
                
                <p>
                    The challenge was not only to address the problem itself, but also to
                    collaborate across two countries and different disciplines. The ECU
                    students had developed the initial business and marketing perspective,
                    while our team was responsible for shaping the concept into a realistic
                    digital product.
                </p>
                
                <p>
                    One of our main challenges was therefore defining the scope. The
                    original problem was broad, so we needed to identify a specific user
                    group, understand their needs and decide what could realistically be
                    developed within the project timeline.
                </p>

            </div>

        </section>

        <section class="project-section">

            <div class="section-number">02</div>

            <div class="section-content">

                <h2>RESEARCH</h2>

                <p>
                    In order to grasp the scope of this wicked problem, a lot of research needed to be done before developing the actual product. Food waste in this particular area was new for my development group, especially how it works in Australia. We began the project by communicating with ECU students about how food waste is handled in different areas of Australia. After a couple of meetings and discussions, we came to the conclusion to focus on small restaurant businesses, such as food trucks — especially in Perth (where the university is located), since it turned out to be a particularly strong market for this kind of solution.

                    <br>
                    <br>
                    The ECU students gave us the information that Western Australia has an active waste-reduction policy environment, including the Beyond Waste 2030 Roadmap, which requires local governments to implement food organics and garden organics (FOGO) systems. The research also showed that most small and medium food-service businesses handle food waste informally, with very little systematic tracking. In fact, only around 2% of hospitality food waste in Australia is currently repurposed, with the rest going to landfill. Most of this waste (around 65%) occurs in the kitchen during preparation, overproduction, and spoilage, while about 30% happens at the consumer plate level. This insight became central to our design direction, since it meant the product needed to focus primarily on back-of-house decisions rather than customer behavior alone.
                     <br>
                     <br>
                     After receiving research from the ECU students, we in Sweden also needed to conduct our own research. We interviewed chefs at restaurants and researched how food waste is handled in Sweden, among other things — all to gather the information we needed to create an application that would actually work in a kitchen. In particular, we wanted a system that could be used by kitchen staff in a real working environment. It shouldn't be something staff try once and then abandon because it takes too much time and effort. Kitchen staff generally work at high speed and don't need something extra to think about.
                     <br>
                     <br>
                     How do restaurants predict demand?
                     <br>
                    When do they decide how much to cook?
                    <br>
                    Who decides?
                    <br>
                    How often do they throw food away?
                </p>
